      "reasoning": "The bridge, although reportedly used on occasion by the ABiH, was not indispensable for military logistics; the asserted advantage of cutting supply routes was speculative and not demonstrated to be concrete. The resulting loss of a unique cultural monument and the isolation of civilians far outweighed any marginal benefit, indicating that the attack was not justified by military necessity. Under art.8(2)(a)(iv) such conduct is punishable as a war crime.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8(2)(a)(iv)",
          "quoted_text": "Extensive destruction and appropriation of property, not justified by military necessity and carried out unlawfully and wantonly;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The attack on the Old Bridge was disproportionate because the anticipated military advantage was not concrete and the damage was clearly excessive, violating Rome Statute art.8(2)(b)(iv).",
      "reasoning": "Proportionality requires that incidental damage not be excessive in relation to the concrete and direct overall military advantage. The HVO’s claim that destroying the bridge would cut ABiH supply lines was not substantiated; the bridge’s primary function was civilian transit and cultural heritage. The total destruction of the bridge and the severe psychological impact on the civilian population far exceeded any marginal tactical benefit, making the attack clearly excessive.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8(2)(b)(iv)",
          "quoted_text": "Intentionally launching an attack in the knowledge that such attack will cause incidental loss of life or injury to civilians or damage to civilian objects or widespread, long-term and severe damage to the natural environment which would be clearly excessive in relation to the concrete and direct overall military advantage anticipated;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The Old Bridge, as a historic monument, was protected under Additional Protocol II art.16, and its intentional destruction was prohibited regardless of any alleged military use.",
      "reasoning": "AP II art.16 expressly prohibits acts of hostility directed against historic monuments that constitute the cultural or spiritual heritage of peoples, and also prohibits using such monuments in support of the military effort. The bridge, a sixteenth‑century structure, qualifies as a historic monument under customary definitions of cultural property. Its deliberate destruction for morale‑breaking purposes therefore breached AP II, irrespective of the limited military utility attributed to it.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_ii",
          "article": "16",
          "quoted_text": "it is prohibited to commit any acts of hostility directed against historic monuments, works of art or places of worship which constitute the cultural or spiritual heritage of peoples, and to use them in support of the military effort."
